# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Security headers (spec: docs/architecture/backlog/live-sim-demo.md §4.7).
#
# This file used to be pure cache policy. A page with no credentialed endpoint can shrug
# at that; a page that can SPEND MONEY through a same-origin /api/* route cannot. These
# rules set no Cache-Control at all, so they cannot disturb the cache ordering the rest
# of the file relies on (later rules win — see the note at the bottom).
#
# `connect-src 'self'` is the strongest control here and the reason the CSP earns its
# place: no script on this page can ship anything to a third-party host. It costs nothing
# real — every dependency is vendored locally, and the MQTT Link panel already cannot work
# from an https:// page (bridge.js hardcodes ws://, which a secure page may not open), so
# a self-hoster's broker was never reachable from a Pages deployment anyway.
#
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY *NOT* HERE, and this is not an oversight. §4.7 sketches
# `default-src 'self'`, and that is now what ships for scripts. `style-src` still carries
# `'unsafe-inline'`: every page has a `<style>` block and inline `style=` attributes, and
# the JS animates `el.style.transform` on a rAF loop, which no hash can cover. That is the
# residual gap, and it is a far smaller one — a `style-src` hole cannot load or run code,
# only restyle what is already on the page. The rest of the policy is the subset that is
# verifiably safe for this bundle: no page uses <base>, <object>/<embed> or any iframe,
# and no page loads a subresource from another origin (only anchor hrefs and the canonical
# links point off-site).
#
# 2026-09-03 — WHAT THAT PASS ADDED.
#
# ADDED `script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'` (and `default-src 'self'` behind it). The
# policy above had NO `script-src` and NO `default-src`, so script execution was entirely
# unrestricted: an injected `<script src="https://evil/x.js">` would have run. It cannot
# now — combined with `connect-src 'self'` (which already has a measured kill on this site:
# it is what refused the port-8081 fetch), the loader AND the exfiltration halves of the
# usual XSS payload are both closed. It left `'unsafe-inline'` standing, and called that
# the honest gap. The paragraph below is what closing it actually took.
#
# 2026-09-04 — `'unsafe-inline'` IS GONE FROM `script-src`. Counted, not estimated.
#
# The count that blocked this was FOURTEEN inline `<script>` blocks across five pages
# (sim.html 4, index/setup/cloud 3 each, docs.html 1 — four of them `type="importmap"`).
# THIRTEEN WERE DELETED RATHER THAN HASHED, because a file that does not exist cannot
# drift:
#
#   · the seven ordinary blocks became seven files — `sw-reset.js`, `hud.js`, `rail.js`
#     (sim.html), `home.js` (index), `setup.js`, `cloud.js`, `docs.js`. Each `<script src>`
#     sits in the exact document position its inline block held and carries no
#     defer/async, so execution order is unchanged;
#   · the three `<script type="module">` wire-background blocks differed only in an opacity
#     number, so the number moved to `data-opacity` on the `#wire-bg` element (markup, which
#     CSP does not police) and the three blocks became one `wire-bg.js`;
#   · THREE OF THE FOUR IMPORTMAPS WERE DELETED OUTRIGHT. index/setup/cloud carried one
#     solely to resolve the bare specifier in `moxie-wire.js`'s `import … from "three"`.
#     That import now names `./vendor/three/three.module.js` and the maps are gone.
#
# ONE inline block remains and it is HASHED: `sim.html`'s `<script type="importmap">`.
# It cannot become a file — `<script type="importmap" src>` was dropped from the spec and
# no browser ships it — and sim.html genuinely needs a map where the other four pages did
# not, because `moxie.js` imports `three/addons/…` and the two VENDORED addon files import
# bare `"three"` themselves. Patching those two vendored files would have taken the count
# to zero; it was rejected, because a future re-vendor of three.js would quietly restore
# the bare specifier and the trap would simply have moved somewhere with no guard on it.
#
# `'unsafe-hashes'` IS NOT HERE AND IS NOT NEEDED, and the earlier note in this file was
# WRONG about why. It claimed TEN inline event-handler attributes (docs.html 9,
# cloud.html 1). There are ZERO. All ten hits are `el.onclick = function(){…}` — a function
# OBJECT assigned to a DOM property from inside an already-running script, which is not an
# inline script and which CSP has no opinion about. What CSP does refuse is the ATTRIBUTE
# form `<button onclick="f()">`, `setAttribute("onclick", …)` and `javascript:` URLs; this
# bundle has none of the three, and `build_csp_hashes.py` fails the build if one appears —
# because such a handler would not error, it would simply never fire.
#
# THE HASH IS GENERATED, NEVER TYPED. `sim/tools/build_csp_hashes.py` rewrites the
# `script-src` above from the pages on disk; `--check` proves the committed header still
# matches them, and `sim/tests/test_csp_hashes.py` runs that check in CI on every push.
# This is not ceremony: a hash that drifts from its block does not degrade, it BLANKS THE
# PAGE, and it blanks it in production — until 2026-09-03 no suite in this repo served
# `_headers` at all, so nothing local would have seen it. `sim/test_csp.mjs` now loads all
# five pages under this exact policy, asserts ZERO `securitypolicyviolation` events, and
# drives the docs search, a typed SIM turn and the QR card to prove the pages still WORK
# rather than merely render.
#
# 2026-09-03 (SECOND PASS) — `script-src` ALSO ALLOWS `https://static.cloudflareinsights.com`.
#
# Cloudflare Pages INJECTS its Web Analytics beacon into every HTML response: a
# `<script type="module" src="https://static.cloudflareinsights.com/beacon.min.js/…">` with
# an SRI hash and a real `data-cf-beacon` token. We do not write that tag and cannot edit
# it — the platform adds it after our HTML has left this repo. The policy as first shipped
# refused it, so https://moxie.mattvalancy.com/sim logged
#
#     Loading the script '…/beacon.min.js/…' violates the following Content Security Policy
#     directive: "script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'". The action has been blocked.
#
# on EVERY page load (measured in Chrome against production, 2026-09-03). Functionally
# harmless — the site works, the analytics simply never reported — but a permanent console
# error drowns the next real one, and a quiet console is exactly what found the port-8081
# bug this policy was written for.
#
# The alternative was turning the injection off in the Pages project. Rejected: that edits
# the owner's ACCOUNT SETTINGS rather than our code, and silently removes analytics they may
# want. Allowing one first-party Cloudflare host on a Cloudflare-hosted site is a smaller
# and more legible widening. **DO NOT "TIDY" THIS ENTRY AWAY.** The tag it exists for is not
# in this repo, so nothing here will look broken when it goes — the console will simply
# start shouting again on production, where nobody is watching.
#
# `connect-src` IS DELIBERATELY UNCHANGED, and that was CHECKED, not assumed. The beacon
# reports through `navigator.sendBeacon`, and `beacon.min.js` chooses its endpoint as
# `send.to || (version === undefined ? "https://cloudflareinsights.com/cdn-cgi/rum" : null)`
# and then falls back to the RELATIVE `/cdn-cgi/rum?…`. The injected tag carries
# `"version":"2024.11.0"` and no `send.to`, so the report goes SAME-ORIGIN — which
# `connect-src 'self'` already allows — and the live page loaded with the widened script-src
# produced no connect-src refusal in 9 s of observation. Note the trap should that ever
# change: the REPORT host is the bare `cloudflareinsights.com`, NOT the `static.` one, so
# allowing the script host does not cover it. `sim/test_csp.mjs` block 4 pins both halves —
# the beacon host runs, and the bare sibling host is still refused.
#
# ADDED `Strict-Transport-Security`. NOTE THE TRAP already recorded in this repo (§10
# assumption 27, proven false the hard way in PR #72): `_headers` does NOT apply to Pages
# *Function* responses, so everything in this file covers the STATIC PAGES ONLY.
#
# The `/api/*` half is DONE, and it is not done here: PR #115 sets it in code, as the frozen
# `API_SECURITY_HEADERS` in `functions/api/_lib/envelope.js` — HSTS byte-identical to the
# one above, a JSON-appropriate `default-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'none'`
# and `Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin`. That file is where those headers live and
# where they must be changed.
#
# **DO NOT add HSTS, CSP or CORP to the `/api/*` block at the bottom of this file to "fix"
# the asymmetry.** Every such line would be INERT — see that block's own note, settled by a
# real deploy. Inert lines that look live are the original trap: it is precisely how the two
# spending routes went months appearing to carry a security policy that never applied to
# them. A header that protects an `/api/*` response has to be written in `envelope.js`.
#
# 2026-09-05 — `https://challenges.cloudflare.com` IN THREE DIRECTIVES, FOR THE BOT CONTROL.
#
# Cloudflare Turnstile now guards `POST /api/chat` (`functions/api/_lib/turnstile.js`, and
# `sim/web/turnstile.js` is the browser half). The widget is a third-party script that
# draws inside a third-party iframe and talks to its own origin, so it needs THREE
# allowances and each one fails differently if it is missing:
#
#   · `script-src`  — without it `api.js` never loads. The failure is SILENT in the worst
#     way: no widget renders, `getToken()` resolves `null`, and every send answers "try
#     again" while the console carries one refusal nobody is watching.
#   · `frame-src`   — was `'none'`, and is now EXACTLY this host. Turnstile renders its
#     challenge in an iframe; with `'none'` the script loads, reports no error a page can
#     see, and simply never produces a token. This is the one directive in the policy that
#     had to be LOOSENED rather than extended, and the loosening is one host: no site but
#     this one may be framed, and nothing on this site frames anything else (the paragraph
#     above still holds — no page uses `<object>`/`<embed>`).
#   · `connect-src` — the widget posts its own telemetry and fetches challenge material
#     from its origin. THIS IS THE DIRECTIVE TO BE CAREFUL WITH: `connect-src 'self'` has
#     a MEASURED kill on this site — it is what refused the port-8081 fetch that made the
#     typed turn a dead control — and it is the half of an XSS payload that stops
#     exfiltration. So it is widened by EXACTLY ONE HOST and nothing else, and
#     `sim/test_csp.mjs` block 3 still proves an arbitrary off-origin `fetch` is refused.
#
# WHY NOT self-host the widget script. Because it cannot be self-hosted: Turnstile's
# script must be served from `challenges.cloudflare.com` for the challenge to be valid,
# which is the same "the tag is not in this repo" situation as the analytics beacon above.
#
# HOST-EXACT, LIKE THE BEACON. Note the near-neighbour trap the beacon entry already
# records, because this host has one too: the ALLOWED host is
# `challenges.cloudflare.com`, and a bare `cloudflare.com` or a `challenges.` on any other
# domain is NOT allowed and must not be added "for symmetry". `sim/test_csp.mjs` block 9
# pins all three directives by name and proves a sibling host is still refused.
#
# AND THE SITEKEY IS NOT IN THIS REPO. The widget's public sitekey arrives from the
# deployment at runtime, on `/api/health`'s envelope (`functions/api/_lib/env.js::
# publicTurnstile` argues why). A sitekey written into `sim/web/sim.html` would be THIS
# deployment's sitekey in a public repo, and every fork and every branch preview would
# render a widget bound to a domain list they are not on — which is also why a preview
# enforces nothing: `*.pages.dev` is not an authorized hostname for this widget, so a
# challenge there could never pass and the Turnstile variables are deliberately unset.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: microphone=(self), camera=(), geolocation=()
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'sha256-D2XnioWKWXcIE2epN670TChceWuJD92o/6GT7rIBAsw=' https://static.cloudflareinsights.com https://challenges.cloudflare.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' https://challenges.cloudflare.com; img-src 'self' data: blob:; media-src 'self' data: blob:; worker-src 'self' blob:; frame-src https://challenges.cloudflare.com; frame-ancestors 'none'; form-action 'none'; base-uri 'none'; object-src 'none'

# HTML — always revalidate so a redeploy's new asset references show up immediately.
/
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/*.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# App scripts — change often; must revalidate (the /*.js glob is unreliable on
# Pages, so each app script is listed explicitly. THE LIST IS THE WHOLE MECHANISM: an app
# script missing from it is served with Pages' default caching, so a redeploy can leave a
# visitor running yesterday's file against today's HTML. The eight files below marked NEW
# 2026-09-04 are the inline blocks this bundle used to carry — see the note at the top —
# and `life.js`/`cloud-transport.js` had simply been missed when they were added.)
/moxie.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/bridge.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/audio.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/ambient.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/mic.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/stub.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/qr.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/bg.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/moxie-wire.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/env.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/mode.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/life.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/cloud-transport.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/sw-reset.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/hud.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/rail.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/home.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/setup.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/cloud.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/docs.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/wire-bg.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache
# NEW 2026-09-05 — the browser half of the Turnstile bot control. THE LIST IS THE WHOLE
# MECHANISM (see the note above this block): a script missing from it is served with
# Pages' default caching, so a redeploy could leave a visitor running yesterday's
# `turnstile.js` — one that mints a token for the wrong `action`, or none at all —
# against today's `/api/chat`. `sim/test_csp.mjs` block 9 asserts this entry exists.
/turnstile.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# Rendered imagery + docs bundle — short cache so overwrites propagate fast.
/img/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=300
/docs-bundle/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=300
/ambient.json
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/docs-index.json
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/docs-search.json
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/fixtures/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=300
/sessions/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=300
/audio/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

# Vendored libraries + fonts — content-addressed, safe to cache for a year.
# (Listed AFTER the no-cache rules so it wins for vendored .js. The /api/* block below
#  sets no Cache-Control for these paths, so it cannot disturb that.)
/vendor/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# The Pages Functions (functions/api/*). SETTLED BY A REAL DEPLOY (2026-09-03): this block
# is INERT — `_headers` does not apply to a Pages Function response at all. The preview
# served `/sim.html` with the `/*` block's `Referrer-Policy` (so `_headers` demonstrably
# works on the deployment) and served `/api/health` with NO `Referrer-Policy` whatsoever,
# while `Cache-Control: no-store` and `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` were present —
# and those two are exactly the ones `functions/api/_lib/envelope.js` sets in code. The
# "belt and braces" turned out to be the only belt.
#
# It is kept here as documentation of intent, and because a future Pages change that DID
# start applying it would set the same values. But treat it as having no effect: every
# header an /api/* response needs must be set in `envelope.js`, which is where
# `Referrer-Policy: same-origin` now lives. `sim/tests/test_demo_proxy.mjs` holds the two
# in agreement so this block cannot quietly drift away from the code that is real.
/api/*
  Cache-Control: no-store
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: same-origin
